\section{Reading \code{spam_crop}}{

The ratio meant here is the irrigated yield over the all-rainfed yield,
each yield being production over harvested area. A single code is that
crop's ratio. Joined codes mean one of three things:
\itemize{
\item \code{+} with \code{spam_basis} \code{"direct"}: \code{"pmil+smil"} (millet) and
\code{"acof+rcof"} (coffee). SPAM splits one FAO item in two by national
shares, so the codes are \strong{pooled}: harvested area and production are
summed over both codes per regime, and the ratio is computed from the
sums.
\item \code{+} with \code{spam_basis} \code{"composite_weighted"}: the forage grasses
(\code{"whea+barl+ocer"}, items 638, 639, 645, 651, 996) and forage legumes
(\code{"bean+chic+cowp+pige+lent+opul+rest"}, items 640, 641, 643). Each
code's ratio is computed on its own, and the item's ratio is the
\strong{area-weighted mean of those ratios}, weighted by each crop's SPAM2010
harvested area (irrigated plus rainfed) in the country, or by its global
harvested area where the country grows none of them.
\item \code{|} with \code{spam_basis} \code{"product_dominance"}: Linum (772) and Hemp (776),
\code{"ooil|ofib"}. WHEP books one harvested area for the seed (SPAM \code{ooil})
and the fibre (SPAM \code{ofib}), so \strong{one} of the two is used per country:
the aggregate of whichever product has the larger FAOSTAT production
there.
